@startuml redis-06-failover-clock
!include _style.iuml
title triggerFailover() — what the returned Duration actually measures

participant "your @Test" as T
participant "FailoverHelper" as F
participant "master" as M
participant "sentinel quorum\n(3, quorum 2)" as S
participant "replica1" as R

T -> F ++: cluster.getControl().triggerFailover(Duration.ofSeconds(30))
F -> F: findMaster()\n<i>INFO replication over the internal IP —</i>\n<i>the node that reports role:master **now**</i>

F -> M !!: **docker kill → SIGKILL**
note right of M #3a1c1c
  SIGKILL, not SIGTERM. POSIX: SIGKILL
  cannot be caught. No FIN, no ECONNREFUSED —
  just a peer that stops answering, which is
  the case down-after-milliseconds exists for.
  docker stop would test the *wrong* path.
end note

... **down-after-milliseconds = 2000 ms** ...
S -> S: +sdown  <i>(each Sentinel, subjectively)</i>
S -> S: +odown  <i>(quorum 2 agree)</i>
S -> S: leader elected — **majority of all Sentinels**\n<i>+ unique configuration epoch</i>
S -> R: REPLICAOF NO ONE  → **promotion**
S -> S: +switch-master mymaster …

loop every 500 ms, until timeout
  F -> F: roleResolver.clearCache()
  F -> R: INFO replication
  R --> F: **role:master**
end

F --> T --: **Duration** kill → first observation of a new master

note over T #12305a
  assertThat(elapsed).isLessThan(Duration.ofSeconds(15));
  …and then assert what the clock does **not** tell you:
  replication is async, so measure the writes you lost.
end note
@enduml
